	"notes": "This one is a genuine gap worth being upfront about. Mocean is a design agency's homepage, and every picture we took of it — a phone, a laptop, a desktop and even the special \"whole page\" shot — shows nothing but a grainy, colour-shifting gradient with the word \"Mocean\" on it. That is not a broken capture: the page's own code confirms a real header, a row of four service cards, and a six-column footer all exist further down, they are just being held off-screen or invisible by an animation library (GSAP) that only reveals them once a real visitor scrolls the page. Our tool loads the page and waits, but it never scrolls, so it only ever sees the intro. The lesson for anyone reading this record is: for a small number of sites built as one long scroll-triggered animation, a screenshot proves less than it usually does, and the measurements become the more trustworthy half of the evidence, not the picture.",
